__d(function(e,t,a,r,o,i,n){o.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:12,chapter_title:"Electricity and Circuits",estimated_study_time_minutes:50,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Define electric circuit and identify its essential components","Distinguish between a cell and a battery, and identify terminal polarities","Explain the role of a switch in opening and closing a circuit","Differentiate between conductors and insulators with examples","Use a simple circuit tester to classify materials as conductors or insulators","Understand what a short circuit is and why it is dangerous","Recognise the function of a fuse in electrical safety","Describe the difference between series and parallel circuits","Apply electrical safety rules and understand Karnataka's power generation and distribution"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Familiarity with torches, batteries, and light bulbs from daily life","Awareness that electricity is used to power devices at home","Basic understanding of materials \u2014 metals vs. non-metals","Knowledge from Chapter 11 that light can be produced by luminous objects including electric bulbs"],introduction:{why_important:"Electricity powers modern civilisation \u2014 lights, fans, computers, phones, hospitals, trains, and factories all depend on it. Understanding basic circuits, conductors, insulators, and safety is essential life knowledge. This chapter also introduces the concept of electric current \u2014 the foundation for understanding batteries, generators, electronics, and renewable energy, which are critical topics in 21st-century science.",real_life_relevance:"Every time you switch on a light, charge a phone, or use a laptop, you are using an electric circuit. The torch you use during power cuts uses a simple cell-bulb circuit \u2014 exactly what this chapter covers. Understanding why wires must be insulated, why a fuse blows, and how to safely use electricity at home are life skills that this chapter directly provides.",karnataka_context:"Karnataka is one of India's leading states in electricity generation and has pioneered renewable energy. Key Karnataka electricity facts: (1) Sharavathi Hydroelectric Project (Shivamogga) \u2014 1,035 MW from Jog Falls; (2) Raichur Thermal Power Station \u2014 coal-based; (3) Pavagada Solar Park (Tumakuru) \u2014 2,050 MW, one of the world's largest solar parks on 13,000 acres; (4) Kaiga Nuclear Power Plant (Uttara Kannada) \u2014 880 MW nuclear; (5) Wind energy from Chitradurga, Gadag, Belagavi. KPCL (Karnataka Power Corporation Limited) generates, KPTCL transmits, and BESCOM/CESC/GESCOM distribute electricity to consumers. Karnataka set a target of 50% renewable energy by 2030. The state has over 2.5 crore electricity consumers. Bengaluru alone consumes about 4,000 MW daily. Understanding circuits at school leads to careers in electrical engineering, power management, and renewable energy \u2014 all vital for Karnataka's future.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Chapter 11 explained how electric bulbs are luminous \u2014 they produce light. This chapter explains what makes them glow: the flow of electric current through the filament. Chapter 10 introduced measurement; electric quantities like voltage (volts) and current (amperes) also need measurement, using voltmeters and ammeters respectively."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"The Cell",role:"The energy source \u2014 the chemical-energy-to-electrical-energy converter that pushes current around the circuit"},{name:"The Conductor Wire",role:"The path \u2014 the highway on which electric current travels"},{name:"The Bulb",role:"The load \u2014 the device that converts electrical energy to light and heat"},{name:"The Switch",role:"The gatekeeper \u2014 controls whether the circuit is open (no current) or closed (current flows)"},{name:"The Fuse",role:"The protector \u2014 the sacrificial hero that breaks the circuit to prevent fire and damage"}],plot:["The chapter opens with the question: what makes a bulb glow?","It introduces electric current and the need for a closed circuit.","It describes the components of a circuit: cell, wire, bulb, switch.","It explains conductors (allow current) and insulators (block current) and how to test materials.","It covers the short circuit problem and why loads are necessary.","It introduces the fuse as a safety device.","It distinguishes series and parallel circuits and explains why homes use parallel wiring.","It covers electrical safety rules for home and school.","It connects the concepts to Karnataka's power generation and distribution system."],moral:"Electricity is one of humanity's most powerful tools \u2014 but it requires understanding and respect. A simple circuit can power a torch; the same principles, scaled up, power a city. Safety and knowledge go hand in hand."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Electric Circuit and Its Components",simple_explanation:"An electric circuit is a closed path through which current flows. It needs a cell (energy source), wires (path), a load (bulb/device), and a switch (controller). All must be connected without any break for current to flow.",detailed_explanation:"An electric circuit is a complete, closed conducting loop through which electric current (flow of charge) can travel. The four essential components: (1) Cell or battery: converts chemical energy to electrical energy; provides voltage (electrical pressure) to push current; has a positive (+) and negative (\u2212) terminal. Current (conventional) flows from + through the external circuit to \u2212. (2) Conducting wires: provide the path for current; made of copper or aluminium (conductors) coated with plastic/rubber (insulators) for safety. (3) Load (bulb, motor, buzzer): the device that converts electrical energy into another form (light, mechanical energy, sound). The filament of a bulb resists current, heats up, and glows (incandescence). (4) Switch: a device that can break or complete the conducting path. Open switch = broken path = no current = load off. Closed switch = complete path = current flows = load on. A circuit is open if any one connection is broken \u2014 current cannot flow across any gap. The complete circuit must be a closed loop with no breaks.",real_life_example:"A torch is the simplest real electric circuit: cell(s) in the handle (energy source) \u2192 spring and metal casing (wires/conductors) \u2192 switch button (controller) \u2192 bulb (load) \u2192 back to cell. Press the button (close switch) \u2192 circuit closes \u2192 current flows \u2192 bulb glows. Release button (open switch) \u2192 circuit breaks \u2192 current stops \u2192 bulb off. The torch is a pocket-sized demonstration of everything in this chapter.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's street lights in Bengaluru use a similar circuit concept, scaled to city infrastructure: BESCOM's power supply (energy source) \u2192 underground/overhead cables (conductors) \u2192 automatic timers or photocells (switches that turn on at dusk, off at dawn) \u2192 LED street lamps (loads). The entire city's street lighting network is one massive electric circuit with millions of branches. Namma Metro's electric trains also operate on a circuit: the overhead catenary wire (or third rail) delivers current \u2192 pantograph (collector) \u2192 motor circuits \u2192 back through the rails to the substation.",common_mistakes:"Students often think current flows only from the bulb outward \u2014 current flows in a complete loop through all components simultaneously. Also, students sometimes think a battery (multiple cells) and a single cell are the same \u2014 they differ in voltage. Common wiring errors: connecting both wires to the same terminal (both to + or both to \u2212), which creates no potential difference and no current flow.",memory_tip:"Circuit = COMPLETE CIRCLE of current. Like a running track: every runner (electron/current) must have a complete loop to run. Break the track anywhere \u2192 everyone stops. CWLS: Cell, Wire, Load, Switch \u2014 the four circuit members."},{concept_name:"Conductors and Insulators",simple_explanation:"Conductors allow electric current to flow through them (most metals). Insulators do not allow current to flow (rubber, plastic, glass, wood). Wires are conductors coated with insulators for safety. Graphite is a surprising non-metal conductor.",detailed_explanation:"Conductors have free electrons \u2014 electrons that are loosely held by atoms and can move easily through the material when a voltage is applied. Metals are the best conductors because of their 'sea of free electrons'. Best conductors in order: silver (best, too expensive for routine use) \u2192 copper (most common in wiring) \u2192 gold (used in electronics for reliability) \u2192 aluminium (used in high-voltage transmission lines). Insulators have tightly bound electrons \u2014 they resist movement of charge. Common insulators: rubber, plastic, glass, dry wood, dry air, pure water, ceramics, paper. Exceptions to 'all non-metals are insulators': graphite (carbon) conducts because of its layered structure with one free electron per atom; salt water conducts because dissolved salts produce free ions. The material choice in wiring: copper conductor (low resistance, carries current) + PVC plastic or rubber insulation (high resistance, prevents shock and short circuits).",real_life_example:"A simple conductor-insulator tester: take a cell, a bulb, and two wire probes. Touch the probes to the material being tested. Bulb glows = conductor. No glow = insulator. Using this test: iron nail (glow = conductor), rubber eraser (no glow = insulator), copper coin (glow = conductor), plastic scale (no glow = insulator), graphite pencil tip (dim glow = conductor, surprising!), salt water in a glass (glow = conductor, because of dissolved ions), pure distilled water (no glow = insulator \u2014 no ions present).",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's electrical infrastructure makes conductor/insulator distinction critical. KPTCL's 220kV transmission lines use aluminium conductors (lightweight conductor for long spans between towers). The towers themselves are steel (conductor) but the wires are attached via ceramic insulators (porcelain/glass discs that prevent current from flowing down the tower to the ground). Karnataka's Mysuru-based companies manufacture ceramic insulators for India's power grid. Bengaluru's cable manufacturers (many in Peenya industrial area) produce copper and aluminium conductor cables with PVC insulation for building wiring.",common_mistakes:"Students often state 'all metals are conductors and all non-metals are insulators.' While mostly true, graphite (non-metal) is a conductor, and mercury (metal, liquid) is a poor conductor. Also, students confuse salt water (conductor \u2014 has ions) with pure water (insulator \u2014 no ions). The human body is also a conductor because it contains water with dissolved salts \u2014 this is why electric shocks are dangerous.",memory_tip:"METALS CONDUCT (copper, iron, silver, aluminium). NON-METALS INSULATE (rubber, plastic, glass). EXCEPTION: GRAPHITE CONDUCTS. Test with bulb: glow = conductor, dark = insulator. The bulb is your truth meter."},{concept_name:"Safety in Electricity",simple_explanation:"Electricity is dangerous at high voltages (household 230V AC can kill). Always use cells (1.5V DC) for experiments. Key safety rules: dry hands, no overloading, use fuses, licensed electricians for repairs. Fuses protect circuits by melting when current is too high.",detailed_explanation:"Electrical safety hierarchy: Understanding the danger (voltage and current), protective devices (fuses, MCBs, earthing), and safe practices. Voltage danger: India's household supply is 230V AC \u2014 at this voltage, the current through a human body (typical resistance 1,000\u201310,000 ohms) can be 23\u2013230 mA. As little as 10 mA can cause muscle tetanus (inability to let go); 100 mA can cause cardiac arrest; 200+ mA is usually fatal. Cells at 1.5V push too little current through body resistance to be felt. Short circuit dangers: direct connection between + and \u2212 without load \u2192 enormous current \u2192 wire overheats \u2192 fire. Fuse protection: a fuse wire (low melting point alloy, typically rated 1A, 2A, 5A, 13A) melts when current exceeds rating, breaking the circuit. MCBs (Miniature Circuit Breakers) replace fuses in modern homes \u2014 they trip (switch off) automatically and can be reset. Safety practices: dry hands when touching switches, never overload sockets, use appliances with proper earth connections, check for frayed insulation, use licensed electricians for installation/repair.",real_life_example:"Why do electrical fires start? A common cause: a loose wire connection creates a spark, or an overloaded socket (too many appliances) causes overheating. The fuse is designed to blow before the wire temperature reaches the ignition point of nearby materials. In older Bengaluru homes with traditional rewirable fuses, the fuse wire in the main board melts during a short circuit \u2014 the homeowner must replace the fuse wire after finding and fixing the cause. Modern MCBs just need to be switched back on after fixing the fault.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's electricity regulator KERC (Karnataka Electricity Regulatory Commission) mandates safety standards for all electrical installations. BESCOM (Bengaluru Electricity Supply Company) runs electrical safety awareness programmes, especially during the monsoon season (June\u2013September) when wet conditions increase shock risk. Karnataka has seen lightning fatalities in rural areas \u2014 lightning is a massive natural electrical discharge. The state installs lightning conductors (copper rods) on tall buildings to safely conduct lightning strikes to the ground. Farmers in Karnataka's agricultural districts are warned not to work in fields during thunderstorms \u2014 standing in a wet field makes you a conductor between sky and ground.",common_mistakes:"The most critical misconception: students think 1.5V is 'barely any electricity' and household 230V is 'just more of the same.' The danger is not just voltage but the current it can push through your body. 230V through typical body resistance pushes potentially lethal current. 1.5V does not. Also, students sometimes think a fuse 'protects' by blocking current in normal use \u2014 a fuse allows normal current; it only blows (melts) when current exceeds a safe level.",memory_tip:"FUSE = CIRCUIT'S SELF-SACRIFICING HERO. It melts so the rest doesn't burn. Dry hands + electricity = safe. Wet hands + electricity = shock. 1.5V cell = safe to touch. 230V wall = NEVER touch. High voltage = HIGH DANGER."},{concept_name:"Series and Parallel Circuits",simple_explanation:"In a series circuit, components are in one loop \u2014 one break stops everything. In a parallel circuit, components are in separate branches \u2014 one break doesn't affect others. Homes use parallel circuits so each appliance works independently.",detailed_explanation:"Series circuit: components are connected end-to-end in a single loop. The same current flows through all components. If one component fails (opens), the entire circuit is broken \u2014 all components stop. The voltage of the cell is shared among all components (each gets a fraction). Dimmer bulbs when more bulbs are added. Real example: old-style Christmas lights \u2014 one bulb fails \u2192 all go dark. Parallel circuit: components are connected between the same two points (nodes) in separate branches. Each branch carries independent current. If one branch fails, others continue working. Each component receives the full supply voltage \u2014 brightness stays constant regardless of how many bulbs are added (as long as the cell can supply the total current). Real example: home wiring \u2014 switching off the bedroom light doesn't affect the kitchen fan. Homes MUST use parallel because: (1) Each appliance needs full voltage; (2) Each can be switched independently; (3) A fault in one circuit doesn't affect others. Combination circuits (series + parallel together) are used in real electronics.",real_life_example:"Test: in your home, switch off the bedroom light. Does the fan in the hall go off too? No \u2014 they are on parallel circuits. Now imagine if your home was wired in series: switching off any one light would switch off everything. Series is used in some specialised applications (LED strip lights, battery cells in a torch) where the same current through all is desired.",karnataka_example:"Karnataka's power distribution grid is a complex parallel network. Each transformer substation, each street, each home is a separate branch \u2014 connected in parallel across the distribution lines. If one feeder line fails, only that branch loses power; other branches continue. BESCOM's smart grid project in Bengaluru uses digital monitoring to detect faults in specific parallel branches and restore power quickly. The metro's traction power system also uses parallel feeding from multiple substations \u2014 ensuring that one substation failure doesn't stop the entire metro line.",common_mistakes:"Students often think more bulbs in parallel makes each bulb dimmer \u2014 this is a series-circuit property. In parallel, each bulb gets full voltage and shines at full brightness (until the cell is depleted faster). Also, students sometimes confuse 'parallel' with 'two wires running parallel on paper' \u2014 the physical layout on paper doesn't determine series vs. parallel; the circuit topology (how components are connected) does.",memory_tip:"SERIES = one road, one lane. Block one car \u2192 all traffic stops. PARALLEL = multiple lanes. One lane blocked \u2192 others flow free. HOME = PARALLEL (each room independent). TORCH CELLS = SERIES (same current through each cell, voltages add up)."}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Electric Circuit and Its Components",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"Name the four essential components of an electric circuit.",a:"Cell (energy source), conducting wires (path), load/bulb (device), switch (controller)."},{type:"Application",q:"A bulb in a circuit does not glow. The switch is closed. Give two possible reasons.",a:"1. The cell is discharged (dead). 2. The bulb's filament is broken. Also accept: loose wire connection, cell terminals reversed."}]},{after_concept:"Conductors and Insulators",questions:[{type:"Application",q:"A student touches tester probes to a pencil's graphite tip. The bulb glows dimly. Is graphite a conductor or insulator?",a:"Conductor \u2014 the bulb glowing (even dimly) proves current is flowing through the graphite. Graphite is an exception among non-metals \u2014 it conducts electricity due to its free electrons."},{type:"Recall",q:"Name two conductors and two insulators used in household electrical wiring.",a:"Conductors: copper (wire core), aluminium (some cables). Insulators: PVC plastic (wire coating), rubber (insulating material). Also accept ceramic (used in switches and sockets)."}]}],key_takeaways:["An electric circuit must be closed (complete) for current to flow \u2014 any break stops all current.","A cell converts chemical energy to electrical energy; positive terminal = raised bump, negative = flat end.","A battery is two or more cells combined; total voltage = sum of individual cell voltages in series.","Conductors (copper, aluminium, graphite) allow current to flow; insulators (rubber, plastic, glass) block it.","Graphite is an exception: a non-metal that conducts electricity.","A short circuit (direct + to \u2212 connection with no load) causes dangerous overheating \u2014 always include a load.","A fuse protects the circuit by melting when excess current flows, breaking the circuit before damage occurs.","In a series circuit, one break stops all components; in a parallel circuit, other branches continue.","Homes use parallel circuits so each appliance works independently and receives full supply voltage.","Karnataka generates electricity from hydro (Sharavathi), solar (Pavagada), nuclear (Kaiga), thermal (Raichur), and wind (Gadag) sources."],mind_map:{root:"Electricity and Circuits",branches:[{branch:"Circuit Components",sub:["Cell \u2014 energy source (+ and \u2212 terminals)","Wire \u2014 conducting path (copper)","Bulb/Load \u2014 converts electrical energy","Switch \u2014 opens or closes circuit"]},{branch:"Conductors and Insulators",sub:["Conductors \u2014 metals (copper, iron, aluminium)","Insulators \u2014 rubber, plastic, glass, dry wood","Exception: graphite (non-metal conductor)","Salt water: conductor; pure water: insulator"]},{branch:"Circuit Safety",sub:["Short circuit \u2014 direct + to \u2212 without load: dangerous","Fuse \u2014 melts to protect circuit from excess current","MCB \u2014 resettable modern replacement for fuse","Safety rules: dry hands, no overloading, insulated wires"]},{branch:"Series vs Parallel",sub:["Series \u2014 one loop; one break stops all; voltages add","Parallel \u2014 multiple branches; independent; full voltage each","Homes = parallel; torch cells = series"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["Sharavathi HEP \u2014 1035 MW hydro","Pavagada Solar Park \u2014 2050 MW solar","Kaiga NPP \u2014 880 MW nuclear","BESCOM distributes in Bengaluru","KPTCL transmits at high voltage"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 12 covers electricity and circuits. A circuit needs: cell (energy source with + raised and \u2212 flat terminal), wires (conductors), load (bulb), switch (opens/closes circuit). Current flows only in a CLOSED circuit. Conductors allow current (copper, iron, aluminium, graphite, salt water). Insulators block current (rubber, plastic, glass, dry wood, pure water). Graphite = non-metal but conductor (exception). Short circuit = direct + to \u2212 without load \u2192 dangerous overheating. Fuse = safety device that melts when excess current flows, breaking circuit. MCB = modern fuse (resettable). Series circuit: one loop, one break stops all, voltage shared. Parallel circuit: multiple branches, independent, each gets full voltage. Homes use parallel wiring. Karnataka electricity: Sharavathi (hydro, 1035 MW), Pavagada (solar, 2050 MW, Tumakuru), Kaiga (nuclear, 880 MW, Uttara Kannada), Raichur (thermal), Gadag (wind). KPCL generates, KPTCL transmits, BESCOM/CESC/GESCOM distribute. Safety: use only cells for experiments (1.5V safe), NEVER touch household 230V AC wiring.",must_remember_facts:["A circuit must be CLOSED (complete, unbroken loop) for electric current to flow.","Cell: positive terminal = raised bump (+). Negative terminal = flat end (\u2212).","Battery = two or more cells combined. Single cell \u2260 battery (though commonly called one).","Conductors allow current (copper, iron, aluminium). Insulators block current (rubber, plastic, glass).","GRAPHITE (pencil lead) is a non-metal that CONDUCTS electricity \u2014 an important exception.","Short circuit = dangerous direct connection from + to \u2212 without any load \u2192 overheating, fire risk.","Fuse = a thin wire of low melting point that MELTS when excess current flows, protecting the circuit.","Series circuit: one component fails \u2192 ALL stop. Parallel circuit: one fails \u2192 OTHERS continue.","Home wiring uses PARALLEL circuits \u2014 each appliance works independently at full voltage.","Karnataka's Pavagada Solar Park (Tumakuru) is one of the world's largest solar parks at 2,050 MW."]}},2536,[]);